# RFC-0001: Unreal Engine 5 → Creation Kit World Import Framework

> **Status:** Draft

## Vision

> **Build the world in Unreal Engine 5. Build the game in Creation Kit.**

The Unreal Engine 5 → Creation Kit World Import Framework enables Unreal Engine 5 to serve as a modern world editor for Creation Engine projects, while Creation Kit remains responsible for gameplay authoring and final game assembly.

The framework establishes a deterministic pipeline between Unreal Engine 5 and Creation Engine without replacing either tool.

---

# Motivation

Creation Kit was never designed to be a modern large-scale world editing environment.

Although it excels at gameplay authoring, world building and iteration are significantly slower than in contemporary game engines.

This framework moves world creation into Unreal Engine 5 while preserving Creation Kit as the authoritative editor for gameplay systems.

Benefits include:

* Faster landscape creation
* Faster world iteration
* Modern level design workflow
* Native Unreal Engine tools (Landscape, Foliage, Splines, PCG)
* Full compatibility with Creation Engine gameplay systems

---

# Core Principles

## Unreal Engine 5 is the World Editor

Everything related to the physical world is authored in Unreal Engine 5.

This includes:

* Landscape
* Whiteboxing
* Level Design
* Buildings
* Rocks
* Roads
* Rivers
* Foliage
* Markers
* World composition

UE5 is the **single source of truth** for the world.

---

## Creation Kit is the Gameplay Editor

Once a worldspace has been generated, Creation Kit is used for Creation Engine-specific work, including:

* NavMesh
* AI Packages
* Quests
* Dialogue
* Papyrus
* Encounter Zones
* Lighting
* Occlusion
* LOD Generation
* Final Polish

Gameplay authoring remains entirely inside Creation Kit.

---

# Native Compilation

The framework operates entirely within Unreal Engine 5.

Exporting a world does not require Creation Kit to be running.

When the user selects **Export to Creation Engine**, the framework compiles the current UE5 world into a native Creation Engine plugin (`.esm`).

The generated plugin can later be opened directly in Creation Kit as a new worldspace for gameplay authoring, including NavMesh, AI, quests, dialogue, Papyrus, lighting, LOD generation, and final polish.

Creation Kit is not involved in the compilation process.

---
# World Representation

The framework synchronizes only the information required to reconstruct a Creation Engine worldspace.

The canonical world model consists of:

* Asset ID
* Position
* Rotation
* Scale
* Metadata
* Persistent ID
* Landscape

Metadata contains any project-specific information required during import.

Landscape data is read directly from Unreal Engine's native Landscape system and converted into Creation Engine LAND records.

The framework does not introduce a custom terrain format.

---

# Asset Mapping

Unreal Engine does not contain native Creation Engine assets.

Instead, placed objects reference abstract Asset IDs.

Example:

```text
AssetID = ElvenStone01
```

During import:

```text
AssetID
    ↓
STAT
    ↓
ElvenStone01.nif
    ↓
REFR
```

The implementation of the mapping system is intentionally unspecified.

Each project defines its own Asset ID mapping.

---

# Placeholder Workflow

Artists may use any placeholder assets during world creation, including:

* Primitive meshes
* Custom models
* Free assets
* Fab / Marketplace assets
* Quixel assets

The imported data consists only of:

* Transform
* Asset ID
* Metadata
* Persistent ID

Placeholder meshes themselves are never imported.

---

# Asset Agnostic

The framework intentionally makes no assumptions about:

* Asset libraries
* Naming conventions
* Visual style
* Project structure

Asset mapping is entirely project-defined.

---

# Iterative Workflow

The framework is designed for continuous world iteration.

Typical workflow:

1. Modify the world in Unreal Engine 5.
2. Export the project.
3. Generate a new Creation Engine worldspace.
4. Continue gameplay work in Creation Kit.

Throughout production, Unreal Engine 5 remains the single source of truth.

Worldspaces generated by the framework are disposable derived artifacts.

Manual geometry edits performed inside Creation Kit are not preserved after regeneration.

---

# Persistent IDs

Every object owns a stable Persistent ID.

Persistent IDs allow objects to be reliably identified across multiple imports and ensure consistent synchronization between generated worldspaces.

---

# Automatic Cell Assignment

Creation Engine organizes worldspaces into Cells.

Unreal Engine stores objects in continuous world coordinates.

The framework automatically determines the destination Cell for every object based solely on world position.

No manual configuration is required.

Cell assignment is deterministic.

---

# Validation

Before export, the framework validates project data.

Typical validation warnings include:

* Missing Asset ID
* Unknown Asset ID
* Duplicate Persistent ID
* Unsupported object type

Validation reports issues but never modifies project data automatically.

---

# Deterministic Import

The importer reproduces exactly what exists in Unreal Engine.

It never:

* Corrects transforms
* Adjusts scale
* Changes pivots
* Infers user intent
* Repairs authoring mistakes

Project correctness is always the responsibility of the author.

---

# Scope

The framework synchronizes world data only.

It does not modify:

* Quests
* Papyrus
* AI
* Dialogue
* Gameplay logic

---

# Extensibility

The framework is designed to support extensions.

Possible modules include:

* Landscape
* Water
* Roads
* Custom Metadata
* Spawn Points
* Portals
* Trigger Volumes
* Custom Creation Engine record types

